// Rep Max questions: English Literature (AQA 8702), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"gli-001","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sh-mac","lv":1,"q":"When the witches first greet Macbeth, one of their titles is one he already holds. Which?","o":["Thane of Cawdor","Thane of Fife","Thane of Glamis","Prince of Cumberland"],"a":2,"x":"The witches hail him as Thane of Glamis (already his), Thane of Cawdor (given to him soon after) and king hereafter (the prophecy).","w":["Confuses it with the title he gets soon after; he isn't Cawdor yet.","Fife is Macduff's title; the witches don't greet Macbeth with it.","","Prince of Cumberland is the title Duncan gives Malcolm, not a witches' greeting."]}
{"id":"gli-002","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sh-mac","lv":1,"q":"Why does Macbeth hire murderers to kill Banquo?","o":["Banquo refused to fight in the battle against the rebels","Banquo killed King Duncan and Macbeth wants revenge","Lady Macbeth is jealous of Banquo's friendship","The witches said Banquo's descendants would be kings, and Banquo may suspect him"],"a":3,"x":"The witches told Banquo his heirs would be kings, and Macbeth sees Banquo as a threat. Banquo is killed but his son Fleance escapes.","w":["Banquo fought loyally beside Macbeth; he didn't refuse to fight.","Macbeth killed Duncan himself; Banquo is innocent.","No such jealousy in the play; Macbeth acts from the prophecy and fear of Banquo.",""]}
{"id":"gli-003","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sh-mac","lv":2,"q":"In the sleepwalking scene, which action shows Lady Macbeth's guilt?","o":["She sharpens a dagger to kill again","She confesses to Malcolm in front of the court","She kneels at an altar to pray","She rubs her hands as if trying to wash away blood"],"a":3,"x":"Watched by the Doctor and the Gentlewoman, she rubs her hands and speaks of a stain that will not go: guilt over the murders.","w":["Mixes this up with the dagger scene; sleepwalking Lady Macbeth isn't planning murder.","She speaks unknowingly, with only the Doctor and Gentlewoman there, not in court.","She never prays here; the Doctor says she needs God more than a doctor.",""]}
{"id":"gli-004","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sh-mac","lv":3,"q":"Which fact about King James I helps explain why Macbeth features witches and the murder of a king?","o":["James believed in witchcraft and had survived plots against his life","James was descended from Macbeth, so the play honoured his bloodline","James wanted audiences to see rebels as heroes and kings as tyrants","James wanted audiences to believe that witches did not exist"],"a":0,"x":"James wrote a book on witchcraft (Daemonologie, 1597) and survived plots such as the 1605 Gunpowder Plot. He claimed descent from Banquo, not Macbeth.","w":["","James claimed descent from Banquo, not Macbeth, so this is the wrong ancestor.","James feared plots against kings; the play shows regicide as terrible, not heroic.","James believed in witchcraft and wrote a book about it, so the opposite is true."]}
{"id":"gli-005","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-nv-cc","lv":1,"q":"In what order do the three spirits visit Scrooge?","o":["Present, Past, Yet to Come","Yet to Come, Past, Present","Past, Present, Yet to Come","Past, Yet to Come, Present"],"a":2,"x":"Marley's ghost warns Scrooge first. Then come the Ghosts of Christmas Past, Christmas Present and Christmas Yet to Come, in that order.","w":["Reverses the first two; Past comes before Present.","Puts the future ghost first; Yet to Come visits last.","","Swaps the last two; Present comes before Yet to Come."]}
{"id":"gli-006","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-nv-cc","lv":1,"q":"Who is Scrooge's poorly paid clerk, the father of Tiny Tim?","o":["Fred","Bob Cratchit","Mr Fezziwig","Jacob Marley"],"a":1,"x":"Bob Cratchit works for Scrooge for little pay. Fred is Scrooge's nephew, Fezziwig is his old employer and Marley is his dead partner.","w":["Fred is Scrooge's cheerful nephew, not his clerk.","","Fezziwig was Scrooge's kind old employer, not his clerk.","Marley was Scrooge's dead business partner, not Tiny Tim's father."]}
{"id":"gli-007","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-nv-cc","lv":2,"q":"What do Ignorance and Want, the two children beneath the Ghost of Christmas Present's robe, represent?","o":["Scrooge's own lost childhood","The children of Scrooge's nephew Fred","The dangers of a society that neglects its poor children","The children Marley left behind"],"a":2,"x":"Dickens uses them as a warning to Victorian readers: ignoring poverty and lack of education will lead to disaster for society.","w":["Scrooge's younger self appears in the Past scenes; these children are symbols.","They aren't Fred's children; they are symbolic, not family.","","Marley is Scrooge's dead partner; the children are a warning to society."]}
{"id":"gli-008","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-nv-cc","lv":3,"q":"Asked to give to charity in Stave One, Scrooge mentions prisons and workhouses. Which Victorian view does this reflect?","o":["That the state should offer everyone free healthcare","That workhouses were pleasant places to live","That the poor should rely on harsh institutions, not private charity","That poverty had been ended by the 1840s"],"a":2,"x":"After the 1834 Poor Law, many believed the poor should rely on harsh workhouses. Scrooge uses this to avoid giving; Dickens wants readers to question it.","w":["No free state healthcare existed; Scrooge is defending harsh Poor Law institutions.","Workhouses were made grim on purpose to put people off, not pleasant.","","Poverty was widespread in the 1840s; Dickens wrote to expose it."]}
{"id":"gli-009","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-mt-ic","lv":1,"q":"Who is engaged to Sheila Birling at the start of An Inspector Calls?","o":["Gerald Croft","Eric Birling","Arthur Birling","Inspector Goole"],"a":0,"x":"Gerald Croft is Sheila's fiancé; the family are celebrating the engagement at the start. Eric is her brother and Arthur her father.","w":["","Eric is Sheila's brother, not her fiancé.","Arthur is Sheila's father, not her fiancé.","The Inspector is the outsider investigating; he isn't engaged to Sheila."]}
{"id":"gli-010","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-mt-ic","lv":1,"q":"Which family member does Inspector Goole question first?","o":["Sheila Birling","Eric Birling","Arthur Birling","Sybil Birling"],"a":2,"x":"The Inspector shows the photograph to Mr Birling first, who dismissed Eva Smith from his factory. Sheila, Gerald, Mrs Birling and Eric follow.","w":["Sheila is questioned after her father, once Birling has seen the photograph.","Eric is questioned last, after his mother.","","Mrs Birling is questioned in Act 2, after Birling, Sheila and Gerald."]}
{"id":"gli-011","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-mt-ic","lv":2,"q":"Why did Sheila get Eva Smith sacked from the shop Milwards?","o":["Eva Smith had been stealing from the till","Out of jealousy and hurt pride after trying on a dress","Eva Smith had asked for higher wages","Sheila's brother Eric had asked her to"],"a":1,"x":"Sheila was in a furious temper after a dress did not suit her, and she agrees with the Inspector that she was jealous of the pretty shop girl. She feels deep guilt about it.","w":["Eva wasn't accused of stealing; Sheila's reason was personal.","","","Eric had nothing to do with Milwards; Sheila acted from her own jealousy."]}
{"id":"gli-012","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-mt-ic","lv":3,"q":"Why does Priestley write in 1945 a play set in 1912?","o":["He wanted to show 1912 as a happy time worth returning to","He wanted to write about the sinking of the Titanic","He was too young to write about the 1940s","Audiences know what followed, so Birling's confident predictions look foolish"],"a":3,"x":"Priestley uses hindsight: the audience knows war and the Titanic's fate came, so Birling's views are discredited and Priestley's socialist message gains force.","w":["Priestley shows 1912 as complacent and unequal, not as a golden age.","The Titanic is only mentioned in Birling's boasts, not the reason for the setting.","Priestley was in his fifties and had lived through both world wars.",""]}
{"id":"gli-013","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-po-pc","lv":1,"q":"Who wrote Ozymandias, a poem about a ruined statue of a once-great king?","o":["William Blake","Percy Bysshe Shelley","Robert Browning","Alfred, Lord Tennyson"],"a":1,"x":"Shelley's Ozymandias was published in 1818. Blake wrote London, Browning My Last Duchess and Tennyson The Charge of the Light Brigade.","w":["Blake wrote London, a poem about the city's misery, not a ruined statue.","","Browning wrote My Last Duchess, about a duke's portrait, not a ruined statue.","Tennyson wrote The Charge of the Light Brigade, not this sonnet."]}
{"id":"gli-014","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-po-pc","lv":1,"q":"Which poem is spoken by a duke who talks about the portrait of his late wife?","o":["My Last Duchess","Ozymandias","London","Kamikaze"],"a":0,"x":"My Last Duchess is Browning's dramatic monologue; the Duke reveals his controlling, jealous nature as he talks.","w":["","Ozymandias is about a ruined statue of a king, not a duke's portrait.","London is Blake's poem about misery in the city, with no duke in it.","Kamikaze is about a pilot who turns back, not a duke and his wife."]}
{"id":"gli-015","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-po-pc","lv":2,"q":"In Exposure, Wilfred Owen suggests the soldiers' main enemy is what?","o":["Enemy gunfire","The freezing, wet weather","Poison gas attacks","Their own officers' orders"],"a":1,"x":"Owen shows soldiers freezing and waiting in a silent night where 'nothing happens'. Bullets are 'Less deadly than the air' full of snow, so the weather is the greater enemy.","w":["Owen says bullets are 'less deadly than the air', so gunfire is not the main enemy.","","Gas attacks feature in Dulce et Decorum Est, not in Exposure.","Exposure does not blame officers' orders; the poem focuses on nature's cruelty."]}
{"id":"gli-016","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-po-pc","lv":3,"q":"How do Tennyson's Charge of the Light Brigade and Hughes's Bayonet Charge differ in presenting soldiers going into battle?","o":["Tennyson honours courage and duty; Hughes shows a lone soldier's terror","Tennyson shows a lone soldier's terror; Hughes honours a brigade's duty","Both poems present the attack as a glorious victory","Both poems present the soldiers as eager for revenge"],"a":0,"x":"Tennyson praises the six hundred's courage, while Hughes follows one terrified soldier whose patriotic ideals vanish in the attack.","w":["","Swapped the poems: Tennyson's is the brigade's duty, Hughes's the lone terrified soldier.","Hughes shows a terrifying attack, and Tennyson's charge was a deadly blunder.","Neither poem shows soldiers eager for revenge; Tennyson honours duty, Hughes shows fear."]}
{"id":"gli-017","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-un1","lv":1,"q":"How many marks is Question 1 on the unseen poem (Paper 2 Section C) worth?","o":["8","32","40","24"],"a":3,"x":"Q1 on the first unseen poem is 24 marks; Q2, comparing it with a second poem, is 8 marks. Section C totals 32.","w":["8 marks is Question 2, the comparison, not Question 1.","32 is the whole of Section C (24 + 8), not Question 1 alone.","40 is not the mark for any unseen poetry question; Q1 is worth 24.",""]}
{"id":"gli-018","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-un1","lv":2,"q":"What should you do first when you meet an unseen poem?","o":["Read it carefully, noticing the title and what it seems to be about","Go straight to the final stanza and ignore the title","Look only for the rhyme scheme","Guess what the poet's life was like"],"a":0,"x":"Reading closely, more than once, and noticing the title and subject gives you something to build on. Facts about the poet are not given or needed.","w":["","Skipping to the end and ignoring the title throws away clues to the poem's subject.","Rhyme alone misses meaning and ideas; read for the whole poem first.","The poet's life is not given or needed; guessing it is not close reading."]}
{"id":"gli-019","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-un1","lv":2,"q":"Poem lines: \"The playground empties. / One red glove stays on the wall, waiting.\" Which method makes the glove seem lonely?","o":["The rhyme scheme of the two lines","A rhetorical question to the reader","Giving the glove a human feeling with the verb 'waiting'","A simile comparing the glove to the wall"],"a":2,"x":"'Waiting' is personification: only a person can wait. It suggests loss, as if the glove hopes its owner will return. There is no rhyme, question or simile.","w":["The two lines do not rhyme, and rhyme would not make the glove lonely anyway.","There is no question to the reader in these lines.","","There is no simile: nothing is compared using 'like' or 'as'."]}
{"id":"gli-020","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-un1","lv":3,"q":"A poem has three calm four-line stanzas about a garden, then a two-line ending about loss. How does the form support meaning?","o":["The matching stanzas show the poem has no structure","The sudden short ending breaks the calm and makes the loss feel sharp","The short ending shows the poet ran out of ideas","The calm stanzas prove the poem is only about gardening"],"a":1,"x":"A change of form at the end is a structural choice: the shift from calm to brief, pointed lines highlights the speaker's loss.","w":["Matching stanzas are a clear structure, so saying there is none is wrong.","","Short endings are deliberate; this one stresses loss rather than showing a lack of ideas.","The final two lines show the poem is about loss, not just a garden."]}
{"id":"gli-021","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-un2","lv":1,"q":"What does the unseen comparison question (Q2) ask you to do?","o":["Write only about the second poem","Retell what happens in each poem","Give a biography of each poet","Compare how the poets present a shared idea in both poems"],"a":3,"x":"Q2 (8 marks) asks for similarities and differences in the ways the two poets present an idea, using methods such as language and structure from both poems.","w":["Q2 needs both poems, not only the second one.","Retelling the story earns little; you must analyse how ideas are presented.","The poets' lives are not assessed; focus on the poems' methods.",""]}
{"id":"gli-022","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-un2","lv":2,"q":"Which phrase signals a contrast when comparing two poems?","o":["Similarly","In contrast","Likewise","In the same way"],"a":1,"x":"'In contrast' introduces a difference. The other three signal similarity.","w":["'Similarly' signals a likeness, not a difference.","","'Likewise' signals a similarity, not a contrast.","'In the same way' signals a similarity, not a contrast."]}
{"id":"gli-023","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-un2","lv":2,"q":"Poem A: \"The sea sulks, grey and slow.\" Poem B: \"The sea roars, gold and glittering.\" Which comparison is accurate?","o":["A makes the sea energetic and bright, whereas B makes it moody and dull","Both poems make the sea calm and gloomy","A makes the sea moody and dull, whereas B makes it energetic and bright","Neither poet gives the sea any human or animal qualities"],"a":2,"x":"'Sulks' gives the sea a gloomy mood and 'roars' gives it energy; the colours 'grey' and 'gold' also contrast.","w":["Swapped the poems: 'sulks' and 'grey' are in A, 'roars' and 'gold' are in B.","B's sea roars and is gold and glittering, so it is not calm and gloomy.","","'Sulks' and 'roars' both give the sea human or animal qualities."]}
{"id":"gli-024","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-un2","lv":3,"q":"Poem 1 has regular rhyme and equal stanzas. Poem 2 is free verse with uneven lines. Which comparison of form is best?","o":["Poem 1 feels controlled and steady; Poem 2 feels looser and more spontaneous","Poem 1 must be happy, whereas Poem 2 must be sad","Poem 2 has no form, so there is nothing to compare","Poem 2 is about nature because it is free verse"],"a":0,"x":"Regular patterns often create order and control; free verse can create freedom or unease. Form has effects but does not fix a poem's subject or mood.","w":["","Regular or free form does not decide whether a poem is happy or sad.","Free verse is still a form choice, and it has effects you can compare.","Form does not fix subject; free verse can be about anything."]}
{"id":"gli-025","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk1","lv":1,"q":"What does AO1 reward in a literature answer?","o":["Accurate spelling and punctuation only","A personal response supported by well-chosen quotations and references","Knowledge of the writer's historical background","Comments on form and structure only"],"a":1,"x":"AO1 is about reading closely, giving an informed personal response and using textual references. Context is AO3, language and structure AO2, and spelling AO4.","w":["Spelling and punctuation are AO4; AO1 is much broader than accuracy.","","Context is AO3, not AO1.","Form and structure is AO2; AO1 needs a personal response with evidence."]}
{"id":"gli-026","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk1","lv":2,"q":"A student writes: \"Lady Macbeth is cruel.\" What would most improve this AO1 sentence?","o":["Retelling the plot of Act 1","Adding the date the play was written","Adding a short quotation and explaining what it shows","Adding more adjectives such as 'very' and 'really'"],"a":2,"x":"AO1 rewards ideas backed by evidence: a short, well-chosen quotation with an explanation of what it shows about her.","w":["Retelling the plot adds no evidence or explanation of her cruelty.","A date is context (AO3) and does not back up the point about her.","","Intensifiers add emphasis, not evidence or explanation."]}
{"id":"gli-027","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk1","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence shows a personal interpretation rather than retelling?","o":["Marley's ghost arrives wearing chains in Stave One.","Marley's chains are described after he appears.","Dickens wrote about Marley's ghost in 1843.","Marley's chains could suggest he is trapped by the guilt he built in life."],"a":3,"x":"'Could suggest' shows the student exploring a meaning. The others state facts or events.","w":["This only retells an event; no meaning is explored.","This describes what happens in the text without interpreting it.","A fact about publication; context, not a personal interpretation.",""]}
{"id":"gli-028","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk1","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence explores more than one view of Macbeth's responsibility for Duncan's murder?","o":["He is responsible, because he is the one who stabs Duncan.","The witches and his wife push him, yet in the end he chooses to kill.","He is not at all responsible, because the witches made him kill.","Lady Macbeth is to blame, because she mocks his courage."],"a":1,"x":"Only this sentence weighs two views: pressure from the witches and Lady Macbeth, and his own choice. Each of the others gives just one view of who is to blame.","w":["Only one view: it blames Macbeth alone.","","Only one view: it blames the witches and ignores his choice.","Only one view: it blames Lady Macbeth alone."]}
{"id":"gli-029","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk2","lv":1,"q":"Which is a feature of the form of a play, rather than a novel?","o":["Chapters","Stage directions","A first-person narrator","Stanzas"],"a":1,"x":"Plays are written to be performed, so they use stage directions and dialogue. Chapters and narrators belong to novels, stanzas to poems.","w":["Chapters are a feature of novels, not plays.","","A first-person narrator is a novel feature; plays use dialogue.","Stanzas are the verse blocks of poems."]}
{"id":"gli-030","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk2","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence comments on structure rather than language?","o":["The adjective 'bitter' suggests cruelty.","The simile compares the fog to a blanket.","The noun 'chains' suggests being trapped.","The novella's five staves each mark a stage in Scrooge's change."],"a":3,"x":"Structure is how a text is organised: staves, order of events, shifts and endings. The others focus on single words or figures of speech.","w":["This is about a single word choice, so it is language.","A simile is a figure of speech, so it is language, not structure.","'Chains' is a word choice, so it is language rather than structure.",""]}
{"id":"gli-031","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk2","lv":2,"q":"In Macbeth, a storm and strange noises accompany Duncan's murder, reflecting the mood. What is this technique called?","o":["Pathetic fallacy","Dramatic irony","A soliloquy","A foil"],"a":0,"x":"Weather or nature that mirrors human emotion or events is pathetic fallacy. Dramatic irony is when the audience knows more than a character, a soliloquy is a speech alone, and a foil is a contrasting character.","w":["","Dramatic irony is the audience knowing more, not weather matching mood.","A soliloquy is a speech alone on stage, not storm imagery.","A foil is a contrasting character, not weather reflecting mood."]}
{"id":"gli-032","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk2","lv":3,"q":"Birling predicts no war and calls the Titanic unsinkable. What effect does this dramatic irony have?","o":["The audience trusts his views more","The audience feels sympathy for his bad luck","The audience, knowing he is wrong, is likely to distrust his other views","It has no effect because the play is set in the past"],"a":2,"x":"The audience of 1945 knows what happened, so Birling looks foolish and his views, including about responsibility, are discredited.","w":["Backwards: the audience knows he is wrong, so trusts him less.","He looks foolish, not unlucky, so sympathy is not the effect.","","The 1945 audience knows the history, which is what makes the irony work."]}
{"id":"gli-033","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk3","lv":1,"q":"In AO3, what does 'context' mean?","o":["The meanings of difficult words in the text","A plot summary of the whole text","Ideas, beliefs and events of the time the text was written or set","The number of pages in the book"],"a":2,"x":"AO3 asks you to relate a text to the time and ideas around it when this helps explain its meaning.","w":["That is vocabulary or glossary work, not context.","A plot summary is retelling, not context.","","Page count has nothing to do with context."]}
{"id":"gli-034","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk3","lv":1,"q":"When was A Christmas Carol first published?","o":["1812","1865","1899","1843"],"a":3,"x":"Dickens published it in December 1843, in the Victorian era, partly to highlight the plight of the poor.","w":["1812 is Dickens's birth year, not the publication date.","Too late: the novella came out in the early Victorian years.","1899 is far too late; Dickens died in 1870.",""]}
{"id":"gli-035","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk3","lv":2,"q":"What did many people in Shakespeare's time believe about a king?","o":["He was chosen by God, so killing him was a terrible sin","He was elected by the people and could be replaced","He had no connection to religion","He could be removed if he was unpopular"],"a":0,"x":"This is the Divine Right of Kings. It helps explain why Duncan's murder is shown as unnatural and why Macbeth's reign is disordered.","w":["","Kings were not elected; people believed God chose them.","Religion was central: the king was seen as God's chosen representative.","Under Divine Right an unpopular king still could not be removed."]}
{"id":"gli-036","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk3","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence uses context (AO3) well, linking it to meaning?","o":["Dickens shows the Cratchits' poverty to challenge readers who blamed the poor.","Dickens was born in Portsmouth in 1812.","Victorian Britain had a queen called Victoria.","Dickens wrote many books during his life."],"a":0,"x":"Good AO3 answers link a fact about the time to what the writer is doing in the text, rather than adding unconnected facts.","w":["","A biographical fact not linked to the text's meaning.","True, but a stand-alone fact with no link to what Dickens does.","Too vague; no link to the text or its meaning."]}
{"id":"gli-037","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk4","lv":1,"q":"How many marks are given for AO4 (spelling, punctuation, vocabulary) on the Macbeth essay?","o":["8","16","30","4"],"a":3,"x":"AO4 is worth 4 marks on the Shakespeare essay and 4 on the modern text essay; the essay's other 30 marks are AO1 to AO3.","w":["8 is the AO4 total across both essays; the Macbeth essay alone gets 4.","16 is far too many; AO4 adds only 4 marks to the Macbeth essay.","30 is the combined AO1 to AO3 marks, not the AO4 marks.",""]}
{"id":"gli-038","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk4","lv":2,"q":"Which pair of questions carries AO4 marks?","o":["The Macbeth essay and the A Christmas Carol essay","The Macbeth essay and the An Inspector Calls essay","The An Inspector Calls essay and the Power and Conflict comparison","The A Christmas Carol essay and the unseen poetry questions"],"a":1,"x":"AO4 is marked only on the Shakespeare and modern text essays. The 19th-century novel, poetry anthology and unseen poetry answers do not carry AO4 marks.","w":["The Christmas Carol essay has no AO4 marks; only Shakespeare and the modern text do.","","Power and Conflict has no AO4 marks, so this pair is only half right.","Neither the novel nor the unseen poetry carries AO4 marks."]}
{"id":"gli-039","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk4","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence uses apostrophes correctly?","o":["Duncans' sons flee after their fathers murder.","Duncan's sons flee after their fathers' murder.","Duncans sons flee after their father's murder.","Duncan's sons flee after their father's murder."],"a":3,"x":"'Duncan's sons' shows the sons belong to Duncan and 'father's murder' shows the murder of one father.","w":["Duncans' wrongly suggests more than one Duncan, and fathers needs an apostrophe.","One father is meant, so fathers' (plural possessive) is wrong; it should be father's.","Missing apostrophe in Duncans: the sons belong to Duncan, so it needs 's.",""]}
{"id":"gli-040","s":"english_lit","t":"lit-sk4","lv":1,"q":"Which sentence has every word spelled correctly?","o":["Priestley's definite message is that everyone is responsible.","Priestley's definate message is that everyone is responsible.","Priestley's definite message is that everyone is responsable.","Preistley's definite message is that everyone is responsible."],"a":0,"x":"Each wrong option has one error: 'definate', 'responsable' and 'Preistley' (it is Priestley).","w":["","Misspelt 'definate'; the word is 'definite', with an i.","Misspelt 'responsable'; it ends -ible: 'responsible'.","The author's name is spelled Priestley, with i before e: P-r-i-e-s-t-l-e-y."]}
